f opEval : opAlgebra →ₗ[ℂ] ℂ where\n625:theorem opEval_apply (T : opAlgebra) : opEval T = (T : ℂ →L[ℂ] ℂ) 1 := rfl\n627:theorem opEval_nonneg_of_star_mul_self (y : opAlgebra) : 0 ≤ opEval (star y * y) := by\n636:theorem opEval_monotone : @Monotone opAlgebra ℂ opOrder.toPreorder _ opEval.toFun := by\n653:noncomputable def opState : opAlgebra →ₚ[ℂ] ℂ :=\n656:theorem opState_one : opState 1 = 1 := by\n660:theorem opState_continuous : Continuous opState.toLinearMap := by\n670:noncomputable def opStatePreserving : StatePreservingData where\n\n--SOURCE CONTRACT--\n# Manuscript source contract\n\n## Authoritative source\n\n- Manuscript: `sources/manuscript/main.tex`\n- Title in source: *Infinite Magic and the Continuum* (`main.tex:117`)\n- Formalization policy: `AGENTS.md`\n\nThis project does not treat its own Lean files, prior agent prompts, or the\nsurrounding repository as mathematical source authority.\n\n## Source clauses in scope\n\n| Source locator | What the manuscript says | Formalization status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `main.tex:1671-1679` | Defines short-range order through a local symplectic basis, with `W_Gamma = span{z_i : i in P}` and finite support. | Requires a precise Lean model of the infinite Pauli/symplectic data. |\n| `main.tex:1740-1752` | States the short-range regional-algebra theorem, with an asserted factorization `A_Gamma` into a pure-sector type-I factor and a core factor that is finite type I or hyperfinite `II_1`. | Target statement; not established by current Lean artifacts. |\n| `main.tex:1915-1920` | Proposes an adapted symplectic basis and a star-isomorphism from an infinite Pauli algebra to `A_Gamma`. | External assumption/gap until the source construction is formalized. |\n| `main.tex:1927-1931` | Writes a pure/mixed product density state. | Finite density-matrix fragments are formalized; the infinite model is not. |\n| `main.tex:1945-1967` | Proposes the GNS/tensor-factor and type conclusions. | Conditional GNS transport is formalized; analytic product and type conclusions are not. |\n| `main.tex:1970-2004` | Proposes quasi-equivalence and normal-folium transport. | Not formalized. |\n\nThe wording above is a locator-oriented paraphrase, not a replacement for the\nmanuscript.\n\n## Permitted auxiliary assumptions\n\nAny manuscript-facing Lean theorem that needs one of the following must expose\nit in the theorem statement or task packet:\n\n1. a completed C-star or von Neumann algebra model for the infinite Pauli\n   system and its regional algebra;\n2. the asserted star-isomorphism from that model to the regional algebra;\n3. the product-state formula and hypotheses making the GNS representation a\n   spatial tensor product;\n4. the von Neumann closure, normality, and quasi-equivalence assumptions needed\n   to transport the claimed factor type; and\n5. any hyperfiniteness or trace theorem used for the core factor.\n\nNone of these assumptions is supplied merely by the phrase “short-range\norder” in the current manuscript source.\n\n## Trace correction\n\nThe manuscript-facing target must distinguish two cases:\n\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is finite-dimensional, a normalized tracial\n  state can be obtained from the finite matrix trace together with the core\n  trace;\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is infinite-dimensional, the appropriate\n  conclusion is a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight on the `II_infinity`\n  target, not a tracial state.\n\nThis is a correction to the intended theorem contract, not a claim that the\ncurrent manuscript already states or proves the corrected dichotomy.\n\n## Legacy firewall\n\nEarlier gLU/refinement/layer-coherence material is listed in\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`. It is retained, but it is neither a source clause nor an\nimplicit assumption of this formalization.\n\n--MANUSCRIPT LOCATOR--\n    This in turn defines a state on $\\cP_I$\n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\tilde{\\w} = \\w_\\cS \\circ \\a.\n    \\end{equation}\n    For every local product $F \\Subset I$ one has that on operators in $\\cA_F$\n    embedded in $\\cP_I$ that the local density matrix is\n    \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:tilde_omega}\n        \\tilde{\\w} = \\bigotimes_{i \\in (F \\cap P)} \\ket{0}\\bra{0} \\otimes \\bigotimes_{j\n        \\in F \\setminus P}\n        \\frac{\\id_j}{2}.\n    \\end{equation}\n    This can be inferred by checking it on Pauli-words, which form a basis of\n    the operators. We can write a general Pauli word up to a phase via\n    \\begin{equation}\n       O = \\prod_{i \\in F} (X_i)^{a_i} (Z_i)^{b_i}. \n    \\end{equation}\n    If any $a_i,b_i \\neq 0$ for $i \\notin P$ or $a_j \\neq 0$ for $j \\in P$,\n    then $\\tilde{\\w}(O) = 0$, as the resulting operator in $\\cA_\\G$ will have a\n    string that is \\LS{In the symplectic complement??} of $W_\\G$, which are\n    annihilated. If the conditions of the previous sentence are not satisfied,\n    then the operator $\\a(O)$ will have a Pauli string that is inside $W_\\G$,\n    which has expectation values $1$. One can check that the state\n    \\eqref{eq:tilde_omega} has the same expectation value. Since the states\n    agree on a basis of operators, they are the same states. Thus the state\n    on $\\G$ can be represented by a density matrix which is a tensor product of\n    pure states or maximally mixed states. We can then form the GNS\n    Hilbert space $\\cH_{\\tilde{\\w}}$ with representation $\\pi_{\\tilde{\\w}}$ of $\\cP_I$, which is an infinite tensor\n    product that we can decompose into a tensor product \n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\cH_{\\tilde{\\w}} = \\cK \\otimes \\cK',\n    \\end{equation}\n    where $\\cK$ is an (infinite) tensor product with vacuum $\\ket{0}$ and\n    $\\cK'$ is the GNS representation associated to the maximally mixed state.\n    We can thus use the type classification of Araki-Woods factors on\n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\pi_{\\tilde{\\w}}(\\cP_I)'' = \\cB(\\cK) \\bar{\\otimes} \\cR,\n    \\end{equation}\n    which follows from \\LS{CITE TENSOR PRODUCT THM WE PROVED}. Here, the exact\n\n--ADVERSARIAL INSTRUCTIONS--\n# Adversarial Review Protocol\n\nUse this before closing substantive Codex work. The goal is to find defects, not\nto summarize accomplishments.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Identify the artifact types touched: `code`, `math`, `tex`, `computation`,\n   `source-backed`, `workflow`.\n2. Inspect the diff or new artifact against the user's request.\n3. Report findings by severity first. If there are no findings, say so and state\n   the remaining risk.\n\n## Universal Checks\n\n- Did the work answer the actual latest user request?\n- Did it modify unrelated files or broaden scope?\n- Did it overwrite, reformat, or revert pre-existing user changes?\n- Are generated files, caches, logs, plots, PDFs, or build artifacts handled\n  intentionally?\n- Are final claims stronger than the evidence supports?\n- Are verification commands actually relevant to the changed artifact?\n\n## Code Checks\n\n- Does the changed behavior match the stated interface and edge cases?\n- Are tests missing for a changed behavior, bug fix, parser, numerical routine,\n  CLI command, or user-facing workflow?\n- Are errors handled where inputs, files, network calls, or external tools can\n  fail?\n- Is there hidden global state, path sensitivity, nondeterminism, race risk, or\n  stale cache behavior?\n- Did dependency, config, or environment changes alter unrelated workflows?\n\n## Math Checks\n\n- Are all assumptions, domains, signs, conventions, normalizations, and boundary\n  conditions stated before use?\n- Are definitions used consistently throughout the derivation?\n- Is any implication actually reversible, or was a one-way statement used both\n  ways?\n- Are there division-by-zero, invertibility, regularity, convergence, branch-cut,\n  gauge, or boundary-term assumptions?\n- Do limiting cases, dimensions/units, symmetries, and known special cases agree?\n- Are symbolic substitutions justified rather than smuggling in the result?\n- Are claims labeled correctly as proof, derivation, check, conjecture, or\n  numerical evidence?\n\n## TeX And Source-Backed Checks\n\n- Does the document compile if compilation was expected?\n- Are labels, references, citations, bibliography entries, and figure paths valid?\n- Are source-backed claims tied to exact locators when required?\n- Is quoted or paraphrased material clearly separated from the agent's synthesis?\n- Does the exposition define terms before using them in load-bearing statements?\n\n## Computation Checks\n\n- Is the computation reproducible from a recorded command?\n- Are inputs, random seeds, versions, tolerances, and units documented when they\n  affect the result?\n- Are outputs interpreted correctly and not over-promoted beyond the computation?\n- Were independent sanity checks used where feasible: small cases, limiting\n  cases, dimensional analysis, symbolic identities, or alternate algorithms?\n\n## Closeout Template\n\nUse this structure internally, then surface the concise version to the user:\n\n```text\nFindings:\n- [severity] file/path:line - issue and consequence.\n\nChecks run:\n- command -> result.\n\nResidual risk:\n- what was not checked, or why the artifact is not a proof/complete validation.\n\nCleanup:\n- remaining dirty/untracked files and whether they are intentional.\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
